<header class="intro"><p class="eyebrow">LA VUELTA DE MARTÍN FIERRO · CANTO XXXII · 1879</p><h1>Martín Fierro’s Advice to His Sons: <em>The Complete Verses and Their Meaning</em></h1><p class="deck">At the end of a life marked by separation, conflict and loss, Martín Fierro offers counsel to his sons and to Cruz’s son. His words on brotherhood, friendship, honesty and work are among the most remembered lines of Argentina’s gaucho epic—and they carry more meaning when we also remember the mistakes of the man who speaks them.</p></header>
<figure class="hero"><img src="/media/fotografias-v3/consejos-fogon-hijos.webp" width="1280" height="853" alt="Modern editorial reconstruction of Martín Fierro speaking to three young men beside a rural fire" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Modern digital illustration, not an archival picture. The source text below comes directly from the site’s Spanish poem corpus.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="body">
<section class="context"><h2>What happens in Canto XXXII?</h2><p>After reunions and stories of hardship in <em>La vuelta de Martín Fierro</em> (1879), the protagonist addresses the young men before they go their separate ways. He speaks as a father, but also as a man whose own choices have sometimes contradicted the principles he now recommends.</p><p><strong>Reading format:</strong> all {stanzas.length} original Spanish stanzas and {verseCount} verses of Canto XXXII are reproduced in their existing order and original wording. The English interpretations are clearly marked as editorial commentary; they are <strong>not</strong> translations of the lines. Select passages have additional observations, while every stanza remains readable.</p><p>The original text is in <a href="/leer/vuelta/32/" lang="es">our Spanish canto reader</a>. You can also read the <a href="/consejos-martin-fierro/" lang="es">Spanish annotated edition</a>.</p></section>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Jump to an advice passage"><strong>Explore the main advice passages</strong>{topics.map(t=><a href={`#mf-vuelta-e${t.n}`}>{t.label}</a>)}</nav>
<section class="interpretation"><h2>How should we read these counsels?</h2><p>These verses are not a separate book of timeless proverbs detached from their story. The man advising his sons has endured coercion, made friends, lost family connections and committed violence. That history complicates the moral authority of his advice and makes the poem richer.</p><p><strong>Unity</strong> responds to a fractured family. <strong>Work</strong> gains meaning after conscription and dispossession. <strong>Nonviolence</strong> asks the reader to remember the narrator’s earlier actions rather than forget them. Hernández presents a voice that can teach and still be questioned.</p></section>
<h2 id="complete-canto">All the verses: Canto XXXII</h2>
<p class="reading-note">Spanish poem, reproduced without editorial modernization. Notes appear after selected stanzas, never inside the original lines.</p>
<div class="verses">
{stanzas.map((e,i)=><>
<section class="stanza" id={e.id} aria-label={`Original stanza ${e.numero}`} lang="es">
